{"title":"Riverbend Bottling","sub":"Which machine is holding the line back?","type":"Cost & operations","industry":"Beverage production","geo":"United States","diff":1,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Bottleneck analysis · payback","prompt":"Riverbend bottles sparkling water in Ohio for supermarket private labels. Customers want 400,000 cases a year but Riverbend can't keep up, and it's losing orders. The plant manager has proposed a $230k upgrade to the filling machine. The owner asks you to check whether that's the right investment.","clarify":[{"q":"How does the line work?","a":"Four stations in sequence: rinse bottles, fill and cap, label, pack into cases."},{"q":"Operating time?","a":"Two shifts, about 3,800 hours a year."},{"q":"Quality?","a":"97% of cases pass final inspection."},{"q":"Economics?","a":"Each extra case sold earns about $4 of contribution."}],"structure":{"root":"Will the filler upgrade let Riverbend meet demand?","branches":[{"head":"Capacity","items":["Rate of each station","Which is slowest (the bottleneck)","Hours run"]},{"head":"Output","items":["Cases passing inspection","Demand cap"]},{"head":"Economics","items":["Extra cases × contribution","Payback on $230k"]}]},"hypothesis":"A line runs at the speed of its slowest station. I'd check that the filler really is the bottleneck, and what becomes the bottleneck after the upgrade.","questions":[{"q":"Here are the station speeds. How many good cases can the line produce today, and how many orders is Riverbend losing?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"398.836437pt\" height=\"163.444pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 398.836437 163.444\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 163.444 L 398.836437 163.444 L 398.836437 0 L 0 0 L 0 163.444 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"a